{"name":"Nutmeg Wood Finish","hex":"#683600","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nutmeg","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree (Myristica fragrans) cultivated in the East Indies for its spicy seeds.","The aromatic seed of this tree, used as a spice.\n The powdered seed, ready for use.\n A whole nutmeg seed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flavour with nutmeg.","To play the ball between the legs of (an opponent)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nutmeg"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"},{"term":"finish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An end; the end of anything.","A protective coating given to wood or metal and other surfaces."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To complete (something).","To apply a treatment to ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/finish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wood finishing","blocks":[{"text":"Wood finishing refers to the process of refining or protecting a wooden surface, especially in the production of furniture where typically it represents between 5 and 30% of manufacturing costs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Worker_Applying_a_Wood_Finish.jpg/330px-Worker_Applying_a_Wood_Finish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_finishing","wikidata":"Q119172976"},{"title":"Nutmeg","blocks":[{"text":"Nutmeg is the seed, or the ground spice derived from the seed, of several tree species of the genus Myristica; fragrant nutmeg or true nutmeg is a dark-leaved evergreen tree cultivated for two spices derived from its fruit: nutmeg, from its seed, and mace, from the seed covering. It is also a commercial source of nutmeg essential oil and nutmeg butter. The Banda Islands, in Maluku, Indonesia, are the main producer of nutmeg and mace, and the true nutmeg tree is native to the islands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Biji_Pala_Bubuk.jpg/330px-Biji_Pala_Bubuk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nutmeg","wikidata":"Q83165"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Large_open_%E2%80%98pores%E2%80%99_in_a_Eucalyptus_sieberi.jpg/500px-Large_open_%E2%80%98pores%E2%80%99_in_a_Eucalyptus_sieberi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Large_open_%E2%80%98pores%E2%80%99_in_a_Eucalyptus_sieberi.jpg/500px-Large_open_%E2%80%98pores%E2%80%99_in_a_Eucalyptus_sieberi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Large open ‘pores’ in Eucalyptus sieberi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALarge_open_%E2%80%98pores%E2%80%99_in_a_Eucalyptus_sieberi.jp